// kannada-demo — Kannada script typesetting in texish.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/kannada-demo.script
//
// Kannada is written left to right, so it needs none of the bidirectional
// reordering Hebrew and Arabic do. What it needs instead is work inside each
// syllable, and Kannada asks for more of it than any other script texish sets.
//
// It builds downward, as Telugu does: a consonant joined to the next by the
// virama (U+0CCD) is drawn as the ottakshara, a subscript beneath and to the
// right of the base, so the base of a conjunct is the consonant that OPENS it
// rather than the one that closes it. A consonant and its vowel sign commonly
// fuse into a single drawn form as well.
//
// Two things set it apart from Telugu. It forms a reph: a syllable-opening ra
// joined by the virama is lifted out and set after the syllable as the
// arkavattu. And it writes five of its twelve vowel signs as combinations of
// others — the oo sign is the o sign plus a length mark, and the o sign is
// itself an e sign plus a uu sign, so one character in memory reaches the font
// as three glyphs. The engine splits them, moves each part that belongs to the
// base back across whatever is subjoined beneath, and lets the font fuse and
// position the rest.
//
// \font kannada selects the bundled face, in a regular and a bold cut. No \rtl
// is needed — the script is left to right.

\use{document}

\title{The Kannada Script in \TeXish}
\author{The texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{Setting a Kannada paragraph}

Each syllable is shaped on its own and the syllables are set left to right. Within a
syllable the consonants fuse with their vowel signs and the joined ones hang below the
base; between syllables the line breaker finds its breaks at the interword spaces exactly
as for Latin text.

\medskip

{\font kannada 14 regular
ಕನ್ನಡ ಒಂದು ಸುಂದರವಾದ ಭಾಷೆ. ಇದು ಎಡದಿಂದ ಬಲಕ್ಕೆ ಬರೆಯಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ. ಪ್ರತಿ ಅಕ್ಷರ ಒಂದು
ವ್ಯಂಜನ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ ಸ್ವರವನ್ನು ಒಟ್ಟಿಗೆ ಹೊಂದಿರುತ್ತದೆ.
}

\section{A consonant and its vowel fuse}

Where Devanagari sets a letter and hangs a sign beside it, Kannada usually draws the pair
as a single form. This is the consonant \italic{ka} through its vowel signs, and most of
them are one glyph rather than two:

\medskip

{\font kannada 16 regular
ಕ ಕಾ ಕಿ ಕೀ ಕು ಕೂ ಕೆ ಕೇ ಕೈ ಕೊ ಕೋ ಕೌ
}

\section{Subjoined consonants}

A consonant joined to the next by the virama is drawn as the ottakshara beneath the base,
which is the consonant that opens the conjunct. A vowel sign belongs to that base, so it
is moved back across the subscript to reach it --- book, sign, and letter:

\medskip

{\font kannada 16 regular
ಪುಸ್ತಕ   ಚಿಹ್ನೆ   ಅಕ್ಷರ
}

\section{The arkavattu}

A ra that opens a syllable and is joined by the virama is not drawn in place: it is lifted
out and set after the syllable, taking its own room on the line. These are duty, action,
sun, and meaning:

\medskip

{\font kannada 16 regular
ಧರ್ಮ   ಕರ್ಮ   ಸೂರ್ಯ   ಅರ್ಥ
}

\section{Independent vowels}

A vowel that opens a syllable is written with its own independent letter, not a sign:

\medskip

{\font kannada 16 regular
ಅ ಆ ಇ ಈ ಉ ಊ ಎ ಏ ಐ ಒ ಓ ಔ
}

\section{A closing line}

A greeting and the city of Bengaluru, set in the bold cut:

\medskip

{\font kannada 18 bold
ನಮಸ್ಕಾರ! ಇದು ಬೆಂಗಳೂರಿನ ಕನ್ನಡ ಲಿಪಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ಬರೆಯಲಾಗಿದೆ.
}
